  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date federal contract obligations in Hawaii total $2.2B across 19,944 awards, with an average award size of $111K. Obligations fell 60.0% year over year. The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer with $1.7B in obligations, followed by the General Services Administration at $264M and the Department of Homeland Security at $113M. Construction and engineering work lead the market: Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction accounts for $602M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction adds $382M. Engineering Services total $262M and Computer Systems Design Services total $131M. The largest vendors reflect those sectors, with DRAGADOS/HAWAIIAN DREDGING/ORION JV at $354M and NAN INC at $272M. Several professional services and technical firms also appear on the top-vendor list, including LEIDOS, INC. at $83.9M and DELOITTE CONSULTING LLP at $53.1M. These figures are partial for the current fiscal year and represent contract obligations to date in FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Hawaii?",
        "body": "Federal buying in Hawaii is concentrated. The Department of Defense accounts for $1.7B of the $2.2B total. The next two buyers, General Services Administration and Department of Homeland Security, account for $264M and $113M respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What types of work and vendors dominate spending?",
        "body": "Construction and engineering dominate obligations, with Other Heavy and Civil Engineering Construction at $602M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $382M. Major vendors match those sectors, led by DRAGADOS/HAWAIIAN DREDGING/ORION JV at $354M and NAN INC at $272M, while engineering and tech contractors like LEIDOS, INC. at $83.9M and DELOITTE CONSULTING LLP at $53.1M cover services work."
      }
    ]
  },
